The attached image displays confocal microscopy of skeletal muscle 24 hours post-injection, illustrating Nano-11's role in inducing local inflammation and facilitating antigen uptake. The top row, representing Nano-11 + OVA injection, shows widespread infiltration of inflammatory cells, including Mac-2+ monocytes/macrophages (green) and MHCII+ cells (green), which have efficiently phagocytized AF647-labeled Nano-11 (red) and co-localized within them. In stark contrast, the bottom row, depicting soluble OVA injection, reveals minimal cellular recruitment and an absence of Nano-11. These results underscore Nano-11's effectiveness in enhancing antigen delivery to critical immune cells at the injection site, a key mechanism for boosting immune responses.